    private static function labelSheetPages(string $nav): array
    {
        $labelSheetFaqs = [            ['Is the VenQore label sheet generator really free?', 'Yes. Building and downloading a print-ready PDF of text labels is completely free, with no signup and no watermark, for any number of labels within the rate limit.'],            ['What kind of labels can I make with this tool?', 'Any plain-text label: mailing and return-address labels, "Fragile" or "This Side Up" warning labels, folder and binder tabs, name badges, and jar or container labels. It is general-purpose — not tied to a barcode or a price, unlike the Barcode Generator print-sheet or the Price Tag Generator.'],            ['Can I print the same label multiple times?', 'Yes, two ways. Set a quantity on a single row to repeat just that label — handy for a return-address label you need fifty of — or use the whole-sheet "copies" multiplier to repeat the entire batch, useful for printing a full set of folder labels twice.'],            ['Does every label have to say the same thing?', 'No — the default model is the opposite. Each row you add or paste is its own distinct label with up to three lines of text, so you can print a batch of completely different folder tabs or name badges in one PDF, or repeat one label many times using the per-row quantity, or mix both in the same sheet.'],            ['What is the bulk-paste format?', 'Separate each label with a blank line. Within a block, the first line becomes line 1, the second becomes line 2, and the third becomes line 3. Add a line like "x10" at the end of a block to repeat that label 10 times.'],            ['What label sizes are supported?', 'Thermal label sizes (40×30 mm up to 100×50 mm shipping labels) for direct label printers, plus Avery-compatible A4 and Letter sheet grids including a 5160-equivalent address label size (66.7 × 25.4 mm, 30 per sheet).'],            ['How do I avoid label misalignment when printing?', 'Always print at 100% / "Actual size" in your PDF viewer — never "Fit to page" or "Shrink to fit", both of which rescale the PDF and throw off alignment with pre-cut label sheets. Also confirm your printer\'s paper size matches the preset you picked (A4 vs Letter) before printing a full sheet.'],        ];
        return [            'tools.label-sheet' => [                'title'       => 'Free Label Sheet Generator — Printable Text Labels PDF | VenQore',                'description' => 'Create print-ready PDF sheets of general-purpose text labels — addresses, warning labels, folder tabs, name badges. Avery-compatible sizes, free, no signup.',                'keywords'    => 'label sheet generator, avery label template free, address label maker, printable labels pdf, folder label generator, name badge printer, fragile label printer',                'jsonld'      => [                    [                        '@context' => 'https://schema.org',                        '@graph'   => [                            self::softwareApplicationLd(                                'VenQore Label Sheet Generator',                                url('/tools/label-sheet-generator'),                                'Free online label sheet generator that builds a print-ready PDF of general-purpose text labels — mailing addresses, warning labels, folder tabs, name badges — on Avery-compatible A4/Letter sheets or thermal label rolls.'                            ),                            self::breadcrumbLd([                                ['name' => 'Home', 'url' => url('/')],                                ['name' => 'Tools', 'url' => url('/tools')],                                ['name' => 'Label Sheet Generator', 'url' => url('/tools/label-sheet-generator')],                            ]),                            self::faqLd($labelSheetFaqs),                            [                                '@type' => 'HowTo',                                'name'  => 'How to print a sheet of text labels',                                'step'  => [                                    ['@type' => 'HowToStep', 'name' => 'Add your labels', 'text' => 'Enter each label as a row with up to three lines of text, or paste a blank-line-separated list into the bulk paste box to add many at once.'],                                    ['@type' => 'HowToStep', 'name' => 'Set repeats if needed', 'text' => 'Give any row a quantity to repeat just that label multiple times, such as a return-address label you need fifty copies of.'],                                    ['@type' => 'HowToStep', 'name' => 'Choose a label size', 'text' => 'Pick a thermal label size for a direct label printer, or an Avery-compatible A4/Letter grid — including a 5160-equivalent address size — for a standard printer.'],                                    ['@type' => 'HowToStep', 'name' => 'Download and print', 'text' => 'Download the PDF and print at 100% / "Actual size" so the labels line up with your label stock.'],                                ],                            ],                        ],                    ],                ],                'static_html' => '<main style="font-family:system-ui,sans-serif;max-width:760px;margin:2rem auto;padding:0 1rem;line-height:1.6">'                    . '<nav aria-label="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/tools">Tools</a> &rsaquo; Label Sheet Generator</nav>'                    . '<h1>Free Label Sheet Generator</h1>'                    . '<p><strong>The VenQore Label Sheet Generator builds a print-ready PDF sheet of general-purpose text labels.</strong> Unlike the barcode print-sheet or the price tag generator, this tool is pure text — mailing addresses, return addresses, "Fragile" or "This Side Up" warning labels, folder and binder tabs, name badges, jar and container labels. Every row you add or paste — up to three lines of free text — becomes its own distinct label on the sheet, and any label can be repeated as many times as you need. Free, no signup, no watermark.</p>'                    . '<h2>Two ways to add labels</h2>'                    . '<ul><li><strong>Manual entry</strong> — add rows one at a time, each with up to three lines of text, optional center alignment, an optional bold first line, and a per-row quantity.</li>'                    . '<li><strong>Bulk paste</strong> — separate each label with a blank line; within a block the first line becomes line 1, the second becomes line 2, the third becomes line 3, and an optional trailing "x10" line sets that label\'s repeat quantity.</li></ul>'                    . '<h2>Common uses</h2>'                    . '<p>Return-address labels printed dozens of times from one row, mailing address labels where every row is a different recipient, warning labels like FRAGILE or THIS SIDE UP, folder and binder tabs, name badges for events, and jar or container labels for a home or retail kitchen.</p>'                    . '<h2>Label sizes</h2>'                    . '<p>Thermal label sizes from 40×30 mm up to a 100×50 mm shipping label for direct label printers, plus Avery-compatible A4 and Letter sheet grids — including a 5160-equivalent address label size (66.7 × 25.4 mm, 30 per sheet) — for any standard printer.</p>'                    . '<h2>Avoiding label misalignment</h2>'                    . '<p>Always print at 100% / "Actual size" in your PDF viewer, never "Fit to page" — scaling the PDF is the most common cause of labels drifting off pre-cut label stock. Double check the paper size in your printer dialog matches the A4 or Letter preset you selected.</p>'                    . '<h2>How to print a sheet of labels</h2>'                    . '<ol><li>Add labels manually or paste a blank-line-separated list.</li><li>Set a repeat quantity on any row that needs multiple copies.</li><li>Choose a thermal or A4/Letter label size.</li><li>Download the PDF and print at 100% scale.</li></ol>'                    . '<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>'                    . implode('', array_map(fn ($qa) => "<p><strong>{$qa[0]}</strong> {$qa[1]}</p>", $labelSheetFaqs))                    . self::toolCta('text labels')                    . '<p>Related: <a href="/tools/price-tag-generator">Price Tag Generator</a> · <a href="/tools/barcode-generator">Barcode Generator</a></p>'                    . $nav . '</main>',            ],        ];
    }

    private static function marginCalculatorPages(string $nav): array
    {
        $marginFaqs = [            ["What's the difference between margin and markup?", 'Margin is profit as a percentage of the selling price: (price − cost) ÷ price. Markup is profit as a percentage of the cost: (price − cost) ÷ cost. They describe the same dollar profit but divide by a different base, so markup is always the larger percentage of the two for a profitable sale — a 50% margin equals a 100% markup, not a 50% markup.'],            ['How do I calculate my profit margin?', 'Subtract your cost from your selling price to get gross profit, then divide by the selling price and multiply by 100. Example: sell price $100, cost $40 → profit $60 → margin = 60 ÷ 100 × 100 = 60%.'],            ["What's a good profit margin for retail?", 'It varies by category — general retail often runs 20–50% gross margin, grocery is much thinner (often single digits to around 15%), and specialty, apparel or jewelry can run 50–70% or higher. There is no single universal target; compare against your specific category and fixed costs.'],            ['How do I price a product to hit a target margin?', 'Use price = cost ÷ (1 − target margin ÷ 100). For a $40 cost and a 60% target margin: 40 ÷ (1 − 0.60) = 40 ÷ 0.40 = $100.'],            ['How do I price a product to hit a target markup?', 'Use price = cost × (1 + target markup ÷ 100). For a $40 cost and a 150% target markup: 40 × (1 + 1.50) = 40 × 2.50 = $100.'],            ['Is this calculator really free?', 'Yes. Every calculation runs in your browser — there is no server round-trip, no signup and no email required, for both the single-item solver and the bulk product-list mode with CSV export.'],        ];
        return [            'tools.margin-calculator' => [                'title'       => 'Free Profit Margin & Markup Calculator | VenQore',                'description' => 'Solve cost, price, margin % and markup % instantly — enter any two and the rest calculate live. Bulk product-list mode with CSV export. Free, no signup.',                'keywords'    => 'profit margin calculator, markup calculator, margin vs markup, gross margin calculator, retail pricing calculator, markup to margin conversion',                'jsonld'      => [                    [                        '@context' => 'https://schema.org',                        '@graph'   => [                            self::softwareApplicationLd(                                'VenQore Profit Margin & Markup Calculator',                                url('/tools/margin-calculator'),                                'Free online calculator that solves cost, selling price, margin percentage and markup percentage from any two known values, with target-price and bulk product-list modes plus CSV export, entirely client-side.'                            ),                            self::breadcrumbLd([                                ['name' => 'Home', 'url' => url('/')],                                ['name' => 'Tools', 'url' => url('/tools')],                                ['name' => 'Margin & Markup Calculator', 'url' => url('/tools/margin-calculator')],                            ]),                            self::faqLd($marginFaqs),                            [                                '@type' => 'HowTo',                                'name'  => 'How to calculate profit margin and markup',                                'step'  => [                                    ['@type' => 'HowToStep', 'name' => 'Enter any two values', 'text' => 'Type a cost, selling price, margin percentage, or markup percentage into any two of the four fields.'],                                    ['@type' => 'HowToStep', 'name' => 'Read the other two instantly', 'text' => 'The remaining two values are calculated live using margin = (price − cost) ÷ price and markup = (price − cost) ÷ cost.'],                                    ['@type' => 'HowToStep', 'name' => 'Or solve for a target price', 'text' => 'Enter a cost and a target margin or markup percentage to see the exact price needed to hit that goal.'],                                    ['@type' => 'HowToStep', 'name' => 'Price a whole product list', 'text' => 'Switch to bulk mode, paste or type multiple products with cost and price, and export the computed margins and markups as a CSV file.'],                                ],                            ],                        ],                    ],                ],                'static_html' => '<main style="font-family:system-ui,sans-serif;max-width:760px;margin:2rem auto;padding:0 1rem;line-height:1.6">'                    . '<nav aria-label="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/tools">Tools</a> &rsaquo; Profit Margin & Markup Calculator</nav>'                    . '<h1>Free Profit Margin & Markup Calculator</h1>'                    . '<p><strong>Enter any two of cost, selling price, margin percentage or markup percentage and the calculator solves the other two instantly.</strong> It also suggests a price to hit a target margin or markup, and includes a bulk mode for pricing an entire product list at once with CSV export. Everything runs in your browser — free, no signup, no watermark.</p>'                    . '<h2>Margin vs. markup — the difference</h2>'                    . '<p>Both describe the same profit in dollars but divide it by a different base. <strong>Margin</strong> divides profit by the <em>selling price</em>: margin % = (price − cost) ÷ price × 100. <strong>Markup</strong> divides profit by the <em>cost</em>: markup % = (price − cost) ÷ cost × 100. Because the selling price is always higher than the cost on a profitable sale, markup is always the larger percentage of the two.</p>'                    . '<h2>Worked example</h2>'                    . '<p>A product costs $40 and sells for $100. Gross profit is $60. As a margin: $60 ÷ $100 = <strong>60%</strong>. As a markup: $60 ÷ $40 = <strong>150%</strong>. The same $60 profit produces two very different-looking percentages — 60% margin equals 150% markup, not 60% markup.</p>'                    . '<h2>Pricing to a target</h2>'                    . '<p>To hit a target margin, use <strong>price = cost ÷ (1 − margin ÷ 100)</strong>. For a $40 cost and a 60% target margin: 40 ÷ 0.40 = $100. To hit a target markup, use <strong>price = cost × (1 + markup ÷ 100)</strong>. For a $40 cost and a 150% target markup: 40 × 2.50 = $100.</p>'                    . '<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>'                    . implode('', array_map(fn ($qa) => "<p><strong>{$qa[0]}</strong> {$qa[1]}</p>", $marginFaqs))                    . self::toolCta('accurate per-sale margin reporting')                    . '<p>Related: <a href="/tools/invoice-generator">Invoice Generator</a> · <a href="/tools/barcode-generator">Barcode Generator</a></p>'                    . $nav . '</main>',            ],        ];
    }

    // ── Inventory Health Toolkit ─────────────────────────────────────

    private static function inventoryHealthPages(string $nav): array
    {
        $invFaqs = [            ['What is a good inventory turnover rate?', 'It varies heavily by category — grocery and perishables often turn 15-25+ times a year, general retail is commonly in the 4-8 range, and slow-moving categories like furniture or jewelry may turn only 2-4 times a year. There is no single universal target; compare your turnover against your own category history and similar retailers. Very high turnover can also signal understocking and lost sales, so faster is not automatically better.'],            ['How do I calculate safety stock?', 'The simple method is: Safety Stock = (Maximum Daily Sales × Maximum Lead Time Days) − (Average Daily Sales × Average Lead Time Days). It compares worst-case demand-during-lead-time against the average case, and the gap is the buffer stock to hold. More statistically rigorous methods use the standard deviation of demand and lead time combined with a service-level Z-score, but the simple method is easier to use with data most small retailers already have.'],            ['What is GMROI and why does it matter?', 'GMROI (Gross Margin Return on Inventory Investment) measures how many dollars of gross margin are earned per dollar tied up in inventory: GMROI = Gross Margin ($) ÷ Average Inventory Cost Value ($). A GMROI above 1.0 generally means the inventory investment is being recovered with some profit on top; higher is generally better. What counts as "good" varies by industry, so use it to track your own trend rather than a universal benchmark.'],            ['How do I calculate reorder point?', 'Reorder Point = (Average Daily Sales × Supplier Lead Time in Days) + Safety Stock. It is the stock level at which a new purchase order should be placed so replenishment arrives before stock runs out. Example: 20 units/day average sales, a 7-day lead time, and 30 units of safety stock gives a reorder point of (20 × 7) + 30 = 170 units.'],            ['Is this Inventory Health Toolkit really free?', 'Yes. All five calculators — reorder point, safety stock, EOQ, GMROI and inventory turnover — run entirely in your browser with live results. No signup, no email required, nothing is sent to a server.'],        ];
        return [            'tools.inventory-health' => [                'title'       => 'Free Inventory Health Toolkit — EOQ, GMROI & Reorder Point | VenQore',                'description' => 'Five standard retail inventory formulas in one free calculator: reorder point, safety stock, economic order quantity (EOQ), GMROI and inventory turnover. Live results, no signup.',                'keywords'    => 'reorder point calculator, safety stock calculator, economic order quantity calculator, EOQ calculator, GMROI calculator, inventory turnover calculator, days of inventory calculator',                'jsonld'      => [                    [                        '@context' => 'https://schema.org',                        '@graph'   => [                            self::softwareApplicationLd(                                'VenQore Inventory Health Toolkit',                                url('/tools/inventory-health'),                                'Free online calculator bundling five standard retail inventory-management formulas — reorder point, safety stock, economic order quantity (EOQ), GMROI and inventory turnover — with live results, entirely client-side.'                            ),                            self::breadcrumbLd([                                ['name' => 'Home', 'url' => url('/')],                                ['name' => 'Tools', 'url' => url('/tools')],                                ['name' => 'Inventory Health Toolkit', 'url' => url('/tools/inventory-health')],                            ]),                            self::faqLd($invFaqs),                            [                                '@type' => 'HowTo',                                'name'  => 'How to calculate reorder point, safety stock, EOQ, GMROI and inventory turnover',                                'step'  => [                                    ['@type' => 'HowToStep', 'name' => 'Calculate safety stock', 'text' => 'Enter max daily sales, max lead time, average daily sales and average lead time to get your buffer stock: (Max Daily Sales × Max Lead Time) − (Average Daily Sales × Average Lead Time).'],                                    ['@type' => 'HowToStep', 'name' => 'Calculate reorder point', 'text' => 'Enter average daily sales, supplier lead time and safety stock (or carry over the computed safety stock) to get the stock level that should trigger a new order.'],                                    ['@type' => 'HowToStep', 'name' => 'Calculate EOQ', 'text' => 'Enter annual demand, cost per order and annual holding cost per unit to find the order quantity that minimizes total ordering and holding cost.'],                                    ['@type' => 'HowToStep', 'name' => 'Calculate GMROI', 'text' => 'Enter gross margin dollars (or net sales and COGS) plus average inventory cost value to see gross margin return on inventory investment.'],                                    ['@type' => 'HowToStep', 'name' => 'Calculate inventory turnover', 'text' => 'Enter COGS and average inventory value to get the turnover ratio and the equivalent days of inventory on hand (365 ÷ turnover).'],                                ],                            ],                        ],                    ],                ],                'static_html' => '<main style="font-family:system-ui,sans-serif;max-width:760px;margin:2rem auto;padding:0 1rem;line-height:1.6">'                    . '<nav aria-label="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/tools">Tools</a> &rsaquo; Inventory Health Toolkit</nav>'                    . '<h1>Free Inventory Health Toolkit</h1>'                    . '<p><strong>Five standard retail inventory-management formulas in one place: reorder point, safety stock, economic order quantity (EOQ), GMROI and inventory turnover.</strong> Each calculator updates live as you type. Everything runs in your browser — free, no signup, no watermark.</p>'                    . '<h2>Reorder Point</h2>'                    . '<p>Reorder Point = (Average Daily Sales × Lead Time Days) + Safety Stock. Example: 20 units/day, 7-day lead time, 30 units safety stock → (20 × 7) + 30 = <strong>170 units</strong>.</p>'                    . '<h2>Safety Stock</h2>'                    . '<p>Simple method: Safety Stock = (Max Daily Sales × Max Lead Time) − (Average Daily Sales × Average Lead Time). Example: max 35 units/day over a max 10-day lead time, vs. average 20 units/day over a 7-day lead time → (35 × 10) − (20 × 7) = 350 − 140 = <strong>210 units</strong>. A more statistical approach using demand/lead-time standard deviation and a service-level Z-score exists, but this simple method covers most small-retailer cases.</p>'                    . '<h2>Economic Order Quantity (EOQ)</h2>'                    . '<p>EOQ = √((2 × Annual Demand × Order Cost) ÷ Holding Cost per Unit). It minimizes the combined cost of ordering and holding inventory.</p>'                    . '<h2>GMROI</h2>'                    . '<p>GMROI = Gross Margin ($) ÷ Average Inventory Cost Value ($). Above 1.0 generally means the inventory investment is being recovered with profit; "good" varies by industry.</p>'                    . '<h2>Inventory Turnover</h2>'                    . '<p>Turnover = COGS ÷ Average Inventory Value. Days of Inventory = 365 ÷ Turnover — two views of the same underlying efficiency number.</p>'                    . '<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>'                    . implode('', array_map(fn ($qa) => "<p><strong>{$qa[0]}</strong> {$qa[1]}</p>", $invFaqs))                    . self::toolCta('automatic reorder-point alerts per warehouse')                    . '<p>Related: <a href="/tools/stock-count-sheet">Stock Count Sheet</a> · <a href="/tools/margin-calculator">Margin Calculator</a></p>'                    . $nav . '</main>',            ],        ];
    }

    // ── Bulk SKU Generator ─────────────────────────────────────

    private static function skuGeneratorPages(string $nav): array
    {
        $skuFaqs = [            ['What makes a good SKU?', 'A good SKU is short (typically 6–12 characters), consistent in structure across your whole catalogue, and encodes useful lookup information such as category and variant — without relying on characters that are easy to misread, like the letter O versus the digit 0, or the letter I versus the digit 1. It should be unique per sellable variant and stable over time.'],            ['Should a SKU include the price?', 'No. Prices change with promotions, cost inflation, and repricing, but a SKU is meant to be a permanent identifier. Embedding a price means every price change either breaks the SKU or forces you to keep an outdated number in the code. Keep pricing in your POS or catalogue, not in the SKU string.'],            ['How long should a SKU be?', 'Most retailers land on 6–12 characters. Long enough to encode category, variant and a sequence number without collisions, short enough to type, scan, and read on a small shelf tag or barcode label without wrapping. Very long SKUs (20+ characters) are harder to bar-code cleanly and slower to key in manually at the register.'],            ['What is the difference between a SKU, a barcode, and a UPC?', 'A SKU (Stock Keeping Unit) is an internal code your business defines and controls — you choose its structure. A UPC (Universal Product Code) is a standardized, globally unique 12-digit number assigned through GS1 for retail trade, typically used by manufacturers so any store can recognize the same product. A barcode is just the scannable graphic representation of a code — it can encode either a SKU or a UPC (or another symbology like Code 128 or QR). You can print a barcode of your own SKU for internal use; see the free Barcode Generator to turn any SKU into a scannable barcode.'],            ['Can I reuse a SKU after discontinuing a product?', 'It is safer not to. Reusing a retired SKU on a new, different product corrupts historical sales and inventory reports that reference the old SKU, and can cause mismatches if the old code is still printed on returned goods, price tags, or in a customer\'s order history.'],            ['Is this SKU generator really free?', 'Yes. The scheme builder, live preview, bulk generation and CSV export all run in your browser — no signup, no email required, and nothing is sent to a server.'],        ];
        return [            'tools.sku-generator' => [                'title'       => 'Free Bulk SKU Generator | VenQore',                'description' => 'Design a structured SKU naming scheme with category, variant, sequence and random segments, then generate SKUs in bulk for a product list. Free CSV export, no signup.',                'keywords'    => 'sku generator, bulk sku generator, sku naming convention, sku scheme, generate sku codes, sku vs barcode vs upc, product sku creator',                'jsonld'      => [                    [                        '@context' => 'https://schema.org',                        '@graph'   => [                            self::softwareApplicationLd(                                'VenQore Bulk SKU Generator',                                url('/tools/sku-generator'),                                'Free tool to design a structured SKU naming scheme from configurable segments (fixed text, category code, variant code, sequential number, random code, separator) and generate SKUs in bulk for a product list, with live preview, duplicate warnings and CSV export, entirely client-side.'                            ),                            self::breadcrumbLd([                                ['name' => 'Home', 'url' => url('/')],                                ['name' => 'Tools', 'url' => url('/tools')],                                ['name' => 'Bulk SKU Generator', 'url' => url('/tools/sku-generator')],                            ]),                            self::faqLd($skuFaqs),                            [                                '@type' => 'HowTo',                                'name'  => 'How to build a SKU scheme and generate SKUs in bulk',                                'step'  => [                                    ['@type' => 'HowToStep', 'name' => 'Define category and variant codes', 'text' => 'List short codes for your categories (e.g. Shoes → SH) and sizes/variants (e.g. Black → BLK).'],                                    ['@type' => 'HowToStep', 'name' => 'Build the segment scheme', 'text' => 'Add segments in order — fixed text, category code, variant code, a zero-padded sequential number, and/or a random alphanumeric code — and choose a separator.'],                                    ['@type' => 'HowToStep', 'name' => 'Check the live preview', 'text' => 'The tool shows an example SKU as you configure the scheme, e.g. SH-BLK-0001.'],                                    ['@type' => 'HowToStep', 'name' => 'Add products and generate', 'text' => 'Add rows manually or paste a plain list of product names (optionally with category and variant), and a SKU is generated per row, auto-incrementing the sequence.'],                                    ['@type' => 'HowToStep', 'name' => 'Export as CSV', 'text' => 'Download the product name / SKU table as a CSV file directly from your browser.'],                                ],                            ],                        ],                    ],                ],                'static_html' => '<main style="font-family:system-ui,sans-serif;max-width:760px;margin:2rem auto;padding:0 1rem;line-height:1.6">'                    . '<nav aria-label="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/tools">Tools</a> &rsaquo; Bulk SKU Generator</nav>'                    . '<h1>Free Bulk SKU Generator</h1>'                    . '<p><strong>Design a structured SKU naming scheme from configurable segments — fixed text, category code, variant code, a zero-padded sequential number, and a random alphanumeric code — with a live preview, then generate a SKU for every row in a product list.</strong> Add rows manually or paste a plain list of product names. Export the results as CSV. Everything runs in your browser — free, no signup, no email required.</p>'                    . '<h2>What makes a good SKU</h2>'                    . '<p>A good SKU is short (typically 6–12 characters), consistent in structure across the whole catalogue, and encodes useful information such as category and variant so staff can recognize a product from the code alone. Avoid characters that are easy to misread when handwritten or scanned in poor light, such as the letter O versus the digit 0, or the letter I versus the digit 1. A SKU should be unique per sellable variant and stable over time — it should never need to change once assigned.</p>'                    . '<h2>Should a SKU include the price?</h2>'                    . '<p>No. Prices change with promotions, cost inflation and repricing, but a SKU is meant to be a permanent identifier. Embedding a price means every price change either breaks the SKU or leaves a stale number baked into the code. Keep pricing in your POS or catalogue, not in the SKU string.</p>'                    . '<h2>SKU vs. barcode vs. UPC — what is the difference?</h2>'                    . '<p>A <strong>SKU</strong> (Stock Keeping Unit) is an internal code your business defines and controls. A <strong>UPC</strong> (Universal Product Code) is a standardized, globally unique 12-digit number assigned through GS1, typically used by manufacturers so any store can recognize the same product. A <strong>barcode</strong> is the scannable graphic representation of a code — it can encode either a SKU or a UPC. You can print a barcode of your own SKU for internal use with the <a href="/tools/barcode-generator">free Barcode Generator</a>.</p>'                    . '<h2>Worked example</h2>'                    . '<p>Scheme: category code + variant code + a 4-digit sequential number, separated by dashes. For "Running Shoe" in category Shoes (SH) and variant Black (BLK), starting the sequence at 1, the generator produces <strong>SH-BLK-0001</strong>; the next row in the same category and variant becomes <strong>SH-BLK-0002</strong>, and so on.</p>'                    . '<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>'                    . implode('', array_map(fn ($qa) => "<p><strong>{$qa[0]}</strong> {$qa[1]}</p>", $skuFaqs))                    . self::toolCta('consistent, collision-free SKUs')                    . '<p>Related: <a href="/tools/barcode-generator">Barcode Generator</a> · <a href="/tools/product-csv-cleaner">Product CSV Cleaner</a></p>'                    . $nav . '</main>',            ],        ];
    }

    // ── QR Code Generator ─────────────────────────────────────

    private static function qrCodePages(string $nav): array
    {
        $qrFaqs = [            ['Is the VenQore QR code generator really free?', 'Yes. Generating and downloading a QR code as PNG or SVG is completely free, with no signup and no watermark.'],            ['What is a QR code error correction level?', 'Error correction lets a QR code still scan correctly even if part of it is damaged, dirty, or covered — for example by a logo. Level L recovers about 7% damage, M about 15%, Q about 25%, and H about 30%. Higher levels make the code slightly denser but far more durable.'],            ['Can I put my logo in the middle of the QR code?', 'Yes. Upload a logo image and it is composited into the center of the code. Because covering the middle removes some of the scannable pattern, logo overlays require High (H) error correction, which the tool switches to automatically so the code stays scannable.'],            ['What content types can I encode?', 'Plain URLs, plain text, WiFi network credentials (scan to auto-join), a vCard contact card, a pre-filled email (mailto with subject/body), and a phone number (tap to call).'],            ['How do WiFi QR codes work?', 'The code encodes the network name, password and encryption type in a standard format that iOS and Android camera apps recognize natively — scanning it offers to join the network directly, with no typing.'],            ['What size should I use for a printed QR code?', 'For anything printed and scanned from a normal reading distance (menus, table cards, flyers), 300–500px at a reasonable print size works well. Larger codes with more margin scan more reliably from further away or in poor lighting.'],            ['PNG or SVG — which should I choose?', 'SVG is vector and scales to any size with no quality loss, ideal for professional printing. PNG is a fixed-resolution raster image, simpler to drop into a document or website. Use SVG for print, PNG for quick sharing or embedding.'],        ];        $useCases = [            ['Restaurant menus', 'Print a QR code linking to your online menu on table tents or window decals — no app required, just point the camera.'],            ['WiFi sharing', 'Put a WiFi QR code on a card at the counter or in a guest room so customers join your network without asking for the password.'],            ['Business cards', 'A vCard QR code lets someone save your contact details to their phone in one scan instead of typing them in.'],            ['Product info & packaging', 'Link a QR code on packaging or a shelf tag to a spec sheet, manual, or product page.'],            ['Marketing & flyers', 'Send print readers straight to a landing page, promotion, or signup form.'],        ];
        $pages = [];
        $pages['tools.qr'] = [            'title'       => 'Free QR Code Generator — URL, WiFi, vCard | VenQore',            'description' => 'Generate free QR codes for URLs, WiFi, contact cards (vCard), email and phone. Custom colors, logo overlay, and PNG/SVG export. No signup, no watermark.',            'keywords'    => 'free qr code generator, qr code maker, wifi qr code generator, vcard qr code, qr code with logo, custom qr code generator',            'jsonld'      => [                [                    '@context' => 'https://schema.org',                    '@graph'   => [                        self::softwareApplicationLd(                            'VenQore QR Code Generator',                            url('/tools/qr-code-generator'),                            'Free online QR code generator supporting URL, plain text, WiFi network, vCard contact, email and phone content types, with custom colors, logo overlay and adjustable error correction.'                        ),                        self::breadcrumbLd([                            ['name' => 'Home', 'url' => url('/')],                            ['name' => 'Tools', 'url' => url('/tools')],                            ['name' => 'QR Code Generator', 'url' => url('/tools/qr-code-generator')],                        ]),                        self::faqLd($qrFaqs),                        [                            '@type' => 'HowTo',                            'name'  => 'How to make a free QR code',                            'step'  => [                                ['@type' => 'HowToStep', 'name' => 'Choose a content type', 'text' => 'Pick URL, text, WiFi, contact card (vCard), email or phone number, and fill in the relevant fields.'],                                ['@type' => 'HowToStep', 'name' => 'Customize the design', 'text' => 'Set foreground and background colors, size, margin, and error correction level. Add a logo if you want one in the center.'],                                ['@type' => 'HowToStep', 'name' => 'Preview live', 'text' => 'The QR code updates automatically as you type or adjust settings — scan it with your phone to confirm before downloading.'],                                ['@type' => 'HowToStep', 'name' => 'Download', 'text' => 'Export as PNG for quick use or SVG for print-quality scaling — free, no signup, no watermark.'],                            ],                        ],                    ],                ],            ],            'static_html' => '<main style="font-family:system-ui,sans-serif;max-width:760px;margin:2rem auto;padding:0 1rem;line-height:1.6">'                . '<nav aria-label="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/tools">Tools</a> &rsaquo; QR Code Generator</nav>'                . '<h1>Free QR Code Generator</h1>'                . '<p><strong>The VenQore QR Code Generator creates free, print-ready QR codes for URLs, plain text, WiFi networks, contact cards (vCard), email and phone numbers.</strong> Customize colors, size, margin and error correction, optionally add a logo in the center, and export as PNG or SVG. No signup, no watermark.</p>'                . '<h2>What is a QR code?</h2>'                . '<p>A QR (Quick Response) code is a two-dimensional barcode that a phone camera can scan to instantly open a link, join a WiFi network, save a contact, or perform another action encoded in the pattern — without typing anything.</p>'                . '<h2>Content types you can encode</h2><ul>'                . '<li><strong>URL</strong> — link to a website, menu, or landing page.</li>'                . '<li><strong>Plain text</strong> — any free-form message.</li>'                . '<li><strong>WiFi network</strong> — SSID, password and encryption type, so scanning joins the network automatically.</li>'                . '<li><strong>vCard contact card</strong> — name, phone, email and company, saved to the scanner\'s contacts in one tap.</li>'                . '<li><strong>Email</strong> — opens a pre-filled email with optional subject and body.</li>'                . '<li><strong>Phone number</strong> — opens the dialer ready to call.</li></ul>'                . '<h2>Error correction levels explained</h2>'                . '<p>Error correction lets a damaged, dirty, or partially covered QR code still scan correctly. <strong>L</strong> (~7% recovery), <strong>M</strong> (~15%), <strong>Q</strong> (~25%) and <strong>H</strong> (~30%) trade a slightly denser pattern for more damage tolerance. Adding a logo to the center covers part of the code, so logo overlays require High (H) error correction to remain reliably scannable.</p>'                . '<h2>Common use cases</h2><ul>'                . implode('', array_map(fn ($u) => "<li><strong>{$u[0]}</strong> — {$u[1]}</li>", $useCases))                . '</ul>'                . '<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>'                . implode('', array_map(fn ($qa) => "<p><strong>{$qa[0]}</strong> {$qa[1]}</p>", $qrFaqs))                . self::toolCta('QR codes')                . '<p>Related: <a href="/tools/barcode-generator">Barcode Generator</a> · <a href="/tools/price-tag-generator">Price Tag Generator</a></p>'                . $nav . '</main>',        ];
        return $pages;    }

    // ── QR Menu Generator ─────────────────────────────────────

    private static function qrMenuPages(string $nav): array
    {
        $menuFaqs = [            ['Is the VenQore QR menu generator really free?', 'Yes. Building a menu, hosting the public menu page, generating its QR code and downloading the printable table cards are all completely free, with no signup and no watermark.'],            ['How is this different from a plain QR code generator?', 'A plain QR code generator just encodes whatever URL or text you give it into an image. This tool builds and hosts the actual menu page for you — categories, items, descriptions and prices — and gives you a QR code that points at that hosted page, so you never have to build or host a menu page yourself.'],            ['Will the QR code keep working after I print it?', 'Yes. The QR code encodes a permanent URL for your menu, not the menu content itself. You can update prices or items any time using your edit link, and every already-printed QR code and table card keeps showing the latest version — no reprinting needed.'],            ['How do I edit my menu after creating it?', 'When you create a menu you get an edit link, shown once. Save it — it is the only way to get back in and make changes, since this free tool has no accounts or logins. Losing the edit link means losing the ability to edit that menu.'],            ['Is contactless QR menu ordering more hygienic than paper menus?', 'Yes — a QR code menu means no shared, handled paper or laminated menu passing between tables and staff, which is why many restaurants adopted them during and after the pandemic and kept using them for the hygiene and cost benefits.'],            ['How much does it cost compared to reprinting laminated menus?', 'A laminated menu reprint costs money and time every time a price or item changes. A QR code menu is edited instantly online at zero cost — you only reprint the small table card (which never has prices on it) if it gets damaged or you change your branding.'],            ['Can I add my logo and brand colors?', 'Yes — upload a logo and pick a theme accent color; both appear on the public menu page and the printable table card.'],            ['What do table card printouts look like?', 'A small printable card sized for a table stand, showing your restaurant name, the QR code and a "Scan for our menu" call to action — no menu items or prices printed on it, since those live on the page the code links to.'],        ];
        $pages = [];
        $pages['tools.qr-menu'] = [            'title'       => 'Free QR Menu Generator — Restaurant QR Menus | VenQore',            'description' => 'Build a free hosted restaurant menu and get a QR code + printable table cards. Edit anytime without reprinting. No signup, no watermark.',            'keywords'    => 'qr menu generator, restaurant qr code menu, contactless menu maker, digital menu qr code, free qr menu maker, table card generator',            'jsonld'      => [                [                    '@context' => 'https://schema.org',                    '@graph'   => [                        self::softwareApplicationLd(                            'VenQore QR Menu Generator',                            url('/tools/qr-menu-generator'),                            'Free QR restaurant menu builder that hosts a public menu page and generates a QR code and printable table cards pointing to it, editable anytime without reprinting.'                        ),                        self::breadcrumbLd([                            ['name' => 'Home', 'url' => url('/')],                            ['name' => 'Tools', 'url' => url('/tools')],                            ['name' => 'QR Menu Generator', 'url' => url('/tools/qr-menu-generator')],                        ]),                        self::faqLd($menuFaqs),                        [                            '@type' => 'HowTo',                            'name'  => 'How to make a free QR code restaurant menu',                            'step'  => [                                ['@type' => 'HowToStep', 'name' => 'Build your menu', 'text' => 'Add categories like Appetizers, Mains and Desserts, then add items with a name, optional description and price to each.'],                                ['@type' => 'HowToStep', 'name' => 'Brand it', 'text' => 'Add your restaurant name, upload a logo, and pick a theme accent color.'],                                ['@type' => 'HowToStep', 'name' => 'Publish', 'text' => 'Save the menu — it is instantly hosted on a public page and a QR code is generated pointing at it.'],                                ['@type' => 'HowToStep', 'name' => 'Print table cards', 'text' => 'Download a printable table card PDF with the QR code, choosing how many copies to print — one per table.'],                                ['@type' => 'HowToStep', 'name' => 'Save your edit link', 'text' => 'Keep the one-time edit link so you can update items or prices later without reprinting a single card.'],                            ],                        ],                    ],                ],            ],            'static_html' => '<main style="font-family:system-ui,sans-serif;max-width:760px;margin:2rem auto;padding:0 1rem;line-height:1.6">'                . '<nav aria-label="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/tools">Tools</a> &rsaquo; QR Menu Generator</nav>'                . '<h1>Free QR Menu Generator</h1>'                . '<p><strong>The VenQore QR Menu Generator builds and hosts a real restaurant menu page for you</strong> — categories, items, descriptions and prices — and gives you a QR code and printable table cards pointing at it. Update your menu any time without reprinting a single card. No signup, no watermark.</p>'                . '<h2>Why a QR code menu instead of a plain QR code?</h2>'                . '<p>A generic QR code generator only turns a URL you already have into an image. This tool is the missing piece before that: it builds and hosts the actual menu page, then wraps a QR code around the URL of that hosted page automatically.</p>'                . '<h2>Benefits of a QR restaurant menu</h2><ul>'                . '<li><strong>Edit without reprinting</strong> — change prices or items instantly online; every printed QR code keeps pointing at the latest version.</li>'                . '<li><strong>Contactless and hygienic</strong> — no shared, handled paper menu passing table to table.</li>'                . '<li><strong>Cost savings</strong> — no laminated menu reprints every time a price changes.</li>'                . '<li><strong>Mobile-first</strong> — the public menu page is built for a phone screen, large tap targets, clean typography.</li></ul>'                . '<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>'                . implode('', array_map(fn ($qa) => "<p><strong>{$qa[0]}</strong> {$qa[1]}</p>", $menuFaqs))                . self::toolCta('QR restaurant menus')                . '<p>Related: <a href="/tools/qr-code-generator">QR Code Generator</a> · <a href="/tools/price-tag-generator">Price Tag Generator</a></p>'                . $nav . '</main>',        ];
        return $pages;    }

    // ── Cash Drawer Count Sheet ─────────────────────────────────────

    private static function cashDrawerPages(string $nav): array
    {
        $cashFaqs = [            ['What is a cash drawer count sheet?', 'A cash drawer count sheet is a printable form used to physically count the cash in a till or register at the end of a shift, broken down by denomination (bills and coins). Staff write in how many of each denomination they counted, then compare the total against the amount the POS expected.'],            ['How do I calculate over/short?', 'Over/Short = Total Counted minus Expected Amount. A positive result means the drawer has more cash than expected ("over"); a negative result means less ("short"). The sheet leaves both the Expected Amount and Total Counted lines blank for staff to fill in by hand after the physical count, with the formula printed as a reminder.'],            ['Should I count the drawer before or after the shift?', 'Both, ideally. Count and record the starting float before the shift begins, then count again at the end of the shift and compare the closing total against expected sales plus the starting float. At minimum, count at every shift change so any variance is caught early and tied to a specific shift and cashier.'],            ['Why does till reconciliation matter?', 'Regular till counts catch cashier errors (giving wrong change), starting-float mistakes, and theft early — before small discrepancies compound into large, hard-to-trace losses. Counting every shift change and having a manager verify also creates accountability, since each sheet is signed by both the cashier and the verifier.'],            ['What counts as an acceptable variance threshold?', 'This varies by business, but many retailers set an internal threshold — often a small fixed dollar amount — below which a variance is treated as normal human error and simply logged, with anything larger triggering a review or write-up. Set a policy that fits your cash volume and risk tolerance; this is general operational guidance, not legal or accounting advice.'],            ['How often should I do a cash drawer count?', 'Every shift change is the standard baseline — whenever one cashier hands the drawer to another, or at store close. Higher-cash-volume registers or locations with a history of shrinkage may warrant counting more often, such as at every break or mid-shift.'],            ['Can I print one sheet for multiple registers?', 'Yes. Choose how many registers or drawers to include (up to 10) and the generator prints one complete count section — with its own denomination table, variance section and signature lines — per register on the same PDF, so a multi-register store can reconcile all its tills on a single printed sheet.'],            ['Does it support currencies other than US Dollars?', 'Yes. Choose USD, EUR, GBP, CAD or AUD and the denomination table adjusts to that currency\'s real coins and notes (for example EUR includes 1c–50c coins plus €1/€2 coins and €5–€500 notes, GBP includes 1p–50p coins plus £1/£2 coins and £5–£50 notes).'],            ['Is the Cash Drawer Count Sheet generator free?', 'Yes. Creating and downloading the PDF is completely free, with no signup and no watermark.'],        ];
        return [            'tools.cash-drawer' => [                'title'       => 'Free Cash Drawer Count Sheet Generator — PDF | VenQore',                'description' => 'Create a free printable cash drawer count sheet PDF for end-of-shift till reconciliation. Denomination rows by currency, multi-register support, Expected vs Counted over/short section. No signup.',                'keywords'    => 'cash drawer count sheet, till reconciliation sheet, cash count sheet template, register count sheet pdf, over short cash sheet, end of shift cash count',                'jsonld'      => [                    [                        '@context' => 'https://schema.org',                        '@graph'   => [                            self::softwareApplicationLd(                                'VenQore Cash Drawer Count Sheet Generator',                                url('/tools/cash-drawer-count-sheet'),                                'Free online cash drawer count sheet generator producing an unwatermarked PDF for end-of-shift till reconciliation, with denomination rows by currency, multi-register support, and a blank Expected vs Counted over/short section.'                            ),                            self::breadcrumbLd([                                ['name' => 'Home', 'url' => url('/')],                                ['name' => 'Tools', 'url' => url('/tools')],                                ['name' => 'Cash Drawer Count Sheet', 'url' => url('/tools/cash-drawer-count-sheet')],                            ]),                            self::faqLd($cashFaqs),                            [                                '@type' => 'HowTo',                                'name'  => 'How to create a free printable cash drawer count sheet',                                'step'  => [                                    ['@type' => 'HowToStep', 'name' => 'Add your store and currency', 'text' => 'Enter your business name, optional logo, and pick your currency (USD, EUR, GBP, CAD or AUD) so the denomination table matches your local coins and notes.'],                                    ['@type' => 'HowToStep', 'name' => 'Add registers', 'text' => 'Choose how many registers or drawers to include (up to 10) and name each one, with shift, counted-by and verified-by fields.'],                                    ['@type' => 'HowToStep', 'name' => 'Download and print', 'text' => 'Download the PDF and print it. Each register prints its own denomination table, Expected vs Counted variance section, and signature lines.'],                                    ['@type' => 'HowToStep', 'name' => 'Count and reconcile by hand', 'text' => 'Staff count each denomination, multiply by the printed value to get the subtotal, sum to a Grand Total Counted, then compare against the Expected Amount from the POS to compute Over/Short.'],                                ],                            ],                        ],                    ],                ],                'static_html' => '<main style="font-family:system-ui,sans-serif;max-width:760px;margin:2rem auto;padding:0 1rem;line-height:1.6">'                    . '<nav aria-label="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/tools">Tools</a> &rsaquo; Cash Drawer Count Sheet</nav>'                    . '<h1>Free Cash Drawer Count Sheet Generator</h1>'                    . '<p><strong>Create a printable, unwatermarked PDF cash drawer count sheet for end-of-shift till reconciliation.</strong> Pick your currency for a correct denomination table (coins and notes), cover up to 10 registers on one sheet, and print blank write-in cells for Count, Subtotal, and the Expected vs Counted over/short calculation. No signup, completely free.</p>'                    . '<h2>What you can customize</h2>'                    . '<ul><li>Business name and logo, saved in your browser for next time</li>'                    . '<li>Currency: USD, EUR, GBP, CAD or AUD, each with a correct real-world denomination list</li>'                    . '<li>1 to 10 registers/drawers per sheet, each with its own name, date, shift, counted-by and verified-by fields</li>'                    . '<li>Blank write-in Count and Subtotal columns per denomination row, plus a Grand Total Counted line</li>'                    . '<li>Expected Amount, Total Counted and Over/Short lines with the formula printed as a reminder</li>'                    . '<li>Notes/Discrepancy Explanation section for write-in explanations</li>'                    . '<li>Page numbering ("Page X of Y") for multi-register, multi-page sheets</li></ul>'                    . '<h2>Why till reconciliation matters</h2>'                    . '<p>Counting the drawer at every shift change catches cashier errors, starting-float mistakes and theft early — before small discrepancies compound into large, hard-to-trace losses. Having both a cashier and a manager sign the sheet creates accountability for each count.</p>'                    . '<h2>How to calculate over/short</h2>'                    . '<p>Over/Short = Total Counted minus Expected Amount. A positive number means the drawer has more cash than the POS expected ("over"); a negative number means less ("short"). Most retailers set an internal acceptable-variance threshold below which a small difference is logged as normal human error, with larger or repeated variances triggering a review.</p>'                    . '<h2>How to create a cash drawer count sheet</h2>'                    . '<ol><li>Add your store name, logo and currency.</li><li>Set the number of registers and fill in shift details for each.</li><li>Download and print the PDF.</li><li>Count cash by denomination, tally by hand, and compare against the expected POS amount.</li></ol>'                    . '<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>'                    . implode('', array_map(fn ($qa) => "<p><strong>{$qa[0]}</strong> {$qa[1]}</p>", $cashFaqs))                    . self::toolCta('till reconciliation reports')                    . '<p>Related: <a href="/tools/stock-count-sheet">Stock Count Sheet</a> · <a href="/tools/receipt-generator">Receipt Generator</a></p>'                    . $nav . '</main>',            ],        ];
    }

    // ── Stock Count Sheet ─────────────────────────────────────

    private static function stockCountPages(string $nav): array
    {
        $stockFaqs = [            ['Is the VenQore stock count sheet really free?', 'Yes. Creating and downloading a PDF stock count sheet is completely free, with no signup, no watermark and no limit on how many products or pages you generate.'],            ['What is a stock count sheet used for?', 'A stock count sheet (also called a stocktake sheet or physical inventory sheet) is a paper form staff use to walk the shop floor or warehouse and write down the actual quantity of each product by hand. Those counted numbers are then compared against what the system thinks is in stock to catch shrinkage, theft, damage or data-entry errors.'],            ['Why do a physical count if I already have a POS system?', 'A POS system tracks what it was told happened — sales, purchases, adjustments. It cannot see theft, breakage, spoilage, miscounts at receiving, or staff picking errors. A physical count is the only way to find the gap between recorded stock and what is actually on the shelf, and it is what an accountant needs to true up your books.'],            ['How do I organize a count by location or aisle?', 'Tag each row with a category, aisle or location label before generating the sheet. The PDF groups and sub-headers rows by that label, so the printed sheet is organized the same way someone physically walks the floor — one section per aisle or storage area, in order.'],            ['How often should I do a physical inventory count?', 'Most small retailers do a full count once or twice a year (often at fiscal year-end) and layer in cycle counts — counting a subset of high-value or fast-moving SKUs weekly or monthly — in between. Higher-shrinkage categories (electronics, alcohol, high-theft items) usually warrant more frequent cycle counts.'],            ['What is the difference between cycle counting and a full count?', 'A full count means counting every single SKU in the store at once, usually done after hours since it can take a whole shift. Cycle counting spreads the work out — you count a rotating slice of your catalog continuously (e.g. one aisle per day) so the whole store gets counted over weeks without ever closing for inventory.'],            ['What is a variance report?', 'A variance report compares the quantity your system expected for each product against what staff actually counted, showing the difference (variance) per item — positive if you have more than expected, negative if less. This sheet leaves the Variance column blank on purpose so you can fill it in once you compare the counted quantities against your system stock afterward.'],            ['Can I show a SKU or barcode column?', 'Yes — toggle the SKU column on to print each product\'s SKU or barcode value as plain text next to its name, which helps staff match physical items to your product list without needing to scan anything.'],            ['Does it support landscape orientation?', 'Yes. Landscape is common for count sheets with many columns or extra room for handwritten notes — toggle between Portrait and Landscape before generating the PDF.'],        ];
